   "dLy": 0.03,
   "zq_ohm": 110.0,
   "source": "Arms synthesised on ZYF300CA for a LEVEL split: 34.11 ohm vertical, 46.65 ohm horizontal, measured at -0.22/-0.09/+0.02 dB imbalance across the band; lengths 1.100 x the quarter wave for each arm's own impedance. dip_trim was 0.4129, the coupler's analytic 9.05 deg quadrature shortfall.  Measured in the array it is better at 0.8258: element 2's worst match improves 3.2 dB and element 1's 1.3 dB, while the worst axial ratio across BOTH elements is unchanged at 6.3 dB because element 2 sets it either way.  So the analytic value was right about the coupler and wrong about the element.  dip_trim, which is all that is left once the two patch modes are degenerate. dLx=dLy: modes.py drove each patch edge alone and found the two directions 0.3 MHz apart -- already even-handed -- but 24.5 MHz high, so both dimensions grow together and there is no differential trim to make. The bare patch wanted +0.060; the full network loads it further, so the array was run at 0.000 / +0.030 / +0.060 and +0.030 won on both counts: worst axial ratio 3.35 dB against 3.63 and 3.82, worst match -10.7 dB against -9.5 and -8.1.",
